== Campaign ==
Lindauer's campaign faltered late in the race as a result of his failure to disclose that his wife, a wealthy [[Chicago]] lawyer, had financed the bulk of his campaign.<ref name="archive.org"/> As a result of this revelation, the Republicans withdrew their support of Lindauer<ref name="auto"/> and backed Robin Taylor, the runner-up in the Republican [[Partisan primary#In the United States|primary]], as a [[write-in candidate]].   Due to the lateness of this change, the Republicans unsuccessfully attempted to obtain a court order to delay the election.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.cnn.com/ALLPOLITICS/stories/1998/10/30/alaska.delay.ap/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060516170122/http://www.cnn.com/ALLPOLITICS/stories/1998/10/30/alaska.delay.ap/|url-status=dead|archive-date=16 May 2006|title=Alaska GOP wants election delayed - October 30, 1998|website=[[CNN]] |date=16 May 2006}}</ref> The collapse of Lindauer's campaign resulted in a three-way split of the Republican vote between him, Taylor, and the Republican Moderate Metcalfe.

Knowles defeated Taylor, his closest opponent, by 33%, the widest margin of victory for a gubernatorial candidate in Alaska history.  Moreover, if Taylor's and Lindauer's totals are added together, Knowles defeated the two Republicans combined by 16% – still the widest margin in Alaska history until 2010.  This was also the first time since 1970 that any candidate won an outright majority of the vote in an Alaska gubernatorial election.

